 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Training Time:** Significantly faster than MLP-based NeRFs. On Mip-NeRF360, training takes approximately 15 minutes [S1]. A fast-train variant scales iteration-related hyperparameters by 0.3 for >3x training speedup [S1].
-*   **Rendering Speed:** Comparable to 3D Gaussian Splatting. On Mip-NeRF360, it achieves 121 FPS [S1]. On Deep Blending, it achieves 366 FPS [S1]. A fast-render variant sets $h_{ss}=1.5$ and $h_{prune}=0.15$ for >2x FPS with moderate quality reduction [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Uses more voxel primitives than Gaussian Splatting, resulting in larger model size and higher GPU memory usage [S1]. However, sparse representation avoids OOM issues associated with uniform high-resolution grids [S1]. LiteVoxel variant reduces peak VRAM by up to 60% while maintaining perceptual quality [S2, unofficial].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   **Mip-NeRF360:** LPIPS 0.185, PSNR 27.33, SSIM 0.822 [S1].
-*   **Tanks-and-Temples:** LPIPS 0.144, PSNR 23.04, FPS 125 [S1].
-*   **Deep Blending:** LPIPS 0.228, PSNR 29.84, FPS 366 [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering speeds, fast training convergence, high visual quality (LPIPS), scalability to higher resolutions via sparsity [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to severe exposure variations in training views, leading to floaters and boundary artifacts [S1]. Higher memory usage compared to Gaussian Splatting [S1]. Performance varies significantly across scenes [S1].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Use when real-time novel-view synthesis and fast training are required.
-*   Use when dealing with unbounded scenes requiring efficient background handling.
-*   Use when high perceptual quality (LPIPS) is prioritized over PSNR.
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Avoid scenes with severe exposure variation in training views.
-*   Avoid if GPU memory is extremely constrained and sparse voxel overhead is prohibitive compared to Gaussian Splatting.
+-   **Time Complexity:** Training time is significantly reduced compared to MLP-based NeRFs. Typical training takes ~15 minutes for 20,000 iterations on standard datasets [S1]. Fast-train variants can achieve >3x training speedup by scaling iteration-related hyperparameters by 0.3 [S1].
+-   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of active voxels, not the full grid resolution. This allows handling of high-resolution details that would cause OOM in uniform grids (e.g., $1024^3$) [S1]. However, it uses more voxel primitives than Gaussian splatting, resulting in larger model size and higher GPU memory usage than 3DGS in some scenes [S1].
+-   **Rendering Performance:** Achieves real-time rendering speeds (FPS 121-125 on Mip-NeRF360, up to 366 on Deep Blending) comparable to 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1]. Fast-render variants with $h_{ss}=1.5$ and $h_{prune}=0.15$ achieve >2x FPS with moderate quality reduction [S1].
+-   **Strengths:** High visual quality (low LPIPS), ability to extract watertight meshes via TSDF fusion and Marching Cubes, no dependency on SfM points for geometry, and efficient handling of unbounded scenes [S1].
+-   **Limitations:** Struggles with scenes having severe exposure variation, leading to floaters and boundary artifacts [S1]. Performance can be scene-dependent due to the variable number of contributing primitives [S1].
+-   **When to use:** When real-time rendering and high geometric fidelity (mesh extraction) are required, and the scene has moderate to high complexity where uniform grids are too large.
+-   **When not to use:** For scenes with extreme photometric variations where floaters become problematic, or when minimal GPU memory is available and the scene is very simple (where dense grids or Gaussians might be more efficient).
 
 ## Background
-Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization, specifically in the form of SVRaster, was introduced in the paper "Sparse Voxels Rasterization: Real-time High-fidelity Radiance Field Rendering" [S1]. It builds on the principles of differentiable volume rendering and hierarchical grid optimization [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. The method addresses the limitations of MLP-based NeRFs and uniform voxel grids by combining sparse data structures with direct rasterization. Extensions like SVRecon [S2, unofficial] and LiteVoxel [S2, unofficial] have been developed to improve surface reconstruction and memory efficiency.
+Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization, specifically in the form of SVRaster, was introduced in the paper "Sparse Voxels Rasterization: Real-time High-fidelity Radiance Field Rendering" [S1]. It emerged as a response to the limitations of both neural field methods (slow training, high memory) and Gaussian splatting (lack of explicit geometry, dependency on SfM). The method leverages the efficiency of sparse voxel structures for rasterization and the flexibility of adaptive octrees for representation. It is used in novel-view synthesis and 3D reconstruction tasks, particularly where mesh extraction is desired [S1].
 
